 5. 🗳️ El-Sayed courts Jewish Democrats 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Abdul El-Sayed, the Democratic Senate nominee from Michigan, speaks to reporters in Lansing, Mich., on Saturday. Photo: Sarah Rice/Bloomberg via Getty Images Michigan Senate hopeful Abdul El-Sayed spent the weekend trying to make amends with the state's Jewish Democrats. But he showed there are limits to how far he's willing to go.

 The big one: Hasan Piker. Why it matters: El-Sayed's appearance before the state party's Jewish caucus was an important early test of whether he can pivot from a primary candidate who electrified the party's progressive wing to a general-election nominee who can win over moderate voters uneasy with his politics, Axios' Mike Zapler writes. 

 🔎 Catch up quick: El-Sayed apologized for his comments after a March attack on a suburban Detroit synagogue. It was attempted by a Lebanese-born man who lost relatives in an Israeli airstrike in Lebanon the week before.

 El-Sayed initially condemned the attack. Then, the next day, he released a four-minute video in which he called it "an evil act of displaced rage" but also said that "hurt people hurt people." What he said: El-Sayed on Saturday said he regretted those March comments.

 "I made a mistake," he told the Jewish caucus. "I should have issued a statement, of course, condemning it. And it just should have been the full stop." "We condemned that terrorist attack. … What I was trying to do is offer context for why it happened, but it wasn't the time for context. And you don't ever want to trample somebody's pain," El-Sayed told reporters afterward. What he didn't say: El-Sayed keeps being asked to repudiate Piker, the incendiary left-wing streamer. He keeps refusing.

 Asked repeatedly by reporters Saturday about Piker, El-Sayed wouldn't engage. "If you want to make this race about a streamer in California, I just think you're missing the boat," he said. He later said he was "not interested in a conversation about a social media person in California." Keep reading . 

 
 
