 6. 💰 AI group plans data-center push in battlegrounds 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Build American AI — an advocacy group tied to pro-AI super PAC Leading the Future — is spending big on the data-center fight, with plans to pour millions of dollars into battleground states after Labor Day, according to an announcement shared first with Axios' Maria Curi. 

 Why it matters: The data-center backlash is becoming a defining issue of the midterms, and supporters of the developments are punching back. Build American AI, a 501(c)(4) nonprofit, has about $50 million cash on hand. The group's campaign will begin in Kansas, Ohio and Wisconsin.

 The money will go toward paid advertising, grassroots engagement, research, earned media and public education. 💡 The message: The U.S. should build the infrastructure needed to lead the world in AI while protecting families, communities and the environment.

 The group said the campaign will expand as state legislatures develop bills for 2027 sessions. The advocacy group is also launching a new super PAC — Building the Future (a 527) — to back state and federal candidates aligned with its agenda and oppose those who aren't. Build American AI is pointing to Colorado, Georgia and Texas as models for how states should approach data-center development:

 Colorado Gov. Jared Polis (D) has been bullish on data centers. Georgia offers data-center tax exemptions. Texas Gov. Greg Abbott (R) has spoken harshly of data centers in recent weeks, including requiring companies to pay their own infrastructure costs and pausing new grid approvals pending an audit. Share this story . 

 
 
